\title{Standard-Part Measurement for Finite Quantum States\\with Surreal Labels and Hyperreal Amplitudes}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{18 July 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop a finite non-Archimedean measurement model in which basis states are indexed by surreal numbers and amplitudes are hyperreal. Exact Born weights therefore take values in an ordered field containing positive infinitesimals, while observed probabilities are defined by applying the real-valued standard-part map. Exact Born weights of every nonzero finite state sum to one. A branch with infinitesimal amplitude has observed probability zero whenever the total squared norm is appreciable. In contrast, ket labels do not influence probabilities: two distinct surreal-labelled branches with the same nonzero amplitude each have exact and observed probability $1/2$, even if one label is infinitesimal. A finite lexicographic model exhibits the same classical shadow: standard part sends a normalized measure with infinitesimal visible atoms to a Dirac measure on its reservoir atom while preserving finite additivity. We give proofs, algorithms, worked examples, limitations, and a program toward finite-dimensional non-Archimedean spectral theory.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Conway's surreal numbers form an ordered field containing the real numbers together with infinite and infinitesimal elements. Their breadth makes them natural labels for an unusually rich spectrum of outcomes. Hyperreal fields provide a complementary resource: arithmetic with nonzero infinitesimals and a standard-part operation sending each finite hyperreal to the unique real number infinitely close to it.

We combine these roles without conflating them. Surreal numbers index basis kets; hyperreal numbers provide amplitudes. A finite state is
\[
 |\psi\rangle=\sum_{s\in S}a_s|s\rangle,
\]
where $S$ is a finite set of surreal labels and $a_s\in\Rstar$. Exact Born weights are hyperreal, and observed probabilities are their standard parts.

The separation between labels and amplitudes is fundamental. The state
\[
 \frac{1}{\sqrt2}|0\rangle+\frac{1}{\sqrt2}|\varepsilon\rangle
\]
has equal probabilities on its two distinct labels even when $\varepsilon$ is an infinitesimal surreal. To make the second branch observationally invisible, the infinitesimal must occur in its amplitude, as in $|0\rangle+\varepsilon|1\rangle$. We prove that the latter state has exact weights $1/(1+\varepsilon^2)$ and $\varepsilon^2/(1+\varepsilon^2)$ but observed probabilities $1$ and $0$.

A discrete analogue gives each of $n$ visible atoms formal weight $\delta$ and a reservoir weight $1-n\delta$. Projection to the dominant coordinate sends every visible atom to observed mass zero and the reservoir to mass one. This clarifies standard part as an observation functional. Our scope is finite; no infinite-dimensional spectral theorem is asserted.

\section{Non-Archimedean preliminaries}
\subsection{Infinitesimal and appreciable elements}
A hyperreal $x$ is \emph{infinitesimal} if $|x|<r$ for every real $r>0$. It is \emph{finite} if $|x|<r$ for some positive real $r$, and \emph{infinite} otherwise. A finite hyperreal is \emph{appreciable} when it is not infinitesimal.

Every finite $x\in\Rstar$ is infinitely close to a unique real number. Its \emph{standard part} $\st(x)$ is that real number. Thus $\st(r)=r$ for real $r$ and $\st(\eta)=0$ for infinitesimal $\eta$. On finite arguments, standard part respects addition and multiplication. If $x$ is appreciable, then $x^{-1}$ is finite and $\st(x^{-1})=\st(x)^{-1}$.

\subsection{Finite states}
Let $\No$ denote the surreal numbers. Only finite supports are used, so every sum is finite.

\begin{definition}[Finite quantum-surreal state]
A finite quantum-surreal state is a finitely supported function $\psi:\No\to\Rstar$. With $a_s=\psi(s)$ and $S=\{s:a_s\ne0\}$, write
\[
 |\psi\rangle=\sum_{s\in S}a_s|s\rangle.
\]
Distinct labels denote independent coordinates, irrespective of their distance or order.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Squared norm and probabilities]
Define
\[
 N(\psi)=\|\psi\|^2=\sum_{s\in S}a_s^2.
\]
For $N(\psi)\ne0$, the exact Born weight is
\[
 \Pexact(s\mid\psi)=\frac{a_s^2}{N(\psi)}.
\]
Whenever this weight is finite, the observed probability is
\[
 \Pobs(s\mid\psi)=\st\!\left(\Pexact(s\mid\psi)\right).
\]
\end{definition}

The model uses ordered-field amplitudes. A complex extension would replace $a_s^2$ by $\overline{a_s}a_s$ and would require corresponding positivity and standard-part structures.

\section{Exact normalization}
\begin{theorem}[Born Normalization]
For every finite state $\psi$ with $N(\psi)\ne0$,
\[
 \sum_{s\in S}\Pexact(s\mid\psi)=1.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Finite distributivity gives
\[
 \sum_{s\in S}\frac{a_s^2}{N(\psi)}
 =\frac{\sum_{s\in S}a_s^2}{N(\psi)}
 =\frac{N(\psi)}{N(\psi)}=1.
\]
No Archimedean property is required.
\end{proof}

This is exact normalization in the enlarged field. A global standard-part normalization result additionally requires finiteness of all normalized weights and finite additivity of standard part; appreciability of the norm is the natural stabilizing assumption.

\section{Invisible infinitesimal amplitudes}
\begin{theorem}[Unobservability of an Infinitesimal Branch]
Let $\psi$ be a finite state and $s$ a label. If $a_s$ is infinitesimal and $N(\psi)$ is appreciable, then
\[
 \Pobs(s\mid\psi)=0.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The square $a_s^2$ is infinitesimal. Appreciability of $N(\psi)$ makes $N(\psi)^{-1}$ finite. Their product $a_s^2N(\psi)^{-1}=\Pexact(s\mid\psi)$ is therefore infinitesimal, and its standard part is zero.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Necessity of appreciability]
Smallness is relational. For a one-branch state $\psi=\eta|s\rangle$ with nonzero infinitesimal $\eta$,
\[
 \Pexact(s\mid\psi)=\frac{\eta^2}{\eta^2}=1.
\]
Thus an infinitesimal amplitude need not be invisible inside an even smaller total scale.
\end{remark}

\section{Label invariance and equal amplitudes}
\begin{lemma}[Two-Branch Norm]
For distinct surreal labels $s\ne t$ and hyperreal amplitudes $a,b$,
\[
 N(a|s\rangle+b|t\rangle)=a^2+b^2.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Distinct labels occupy distinct coordinates, so the finite norm sum has exactly the two displayed contributions.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Equal-Amplitude Exact Weights]
Let $s\ne t$ and $a\ne0$. For $|\phi\rangle=a|s\rangle+a|t\rangle$,
\[
 \Pexact(s\mid\phi)=\Pexact(t\mid\phi)=\frac12.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The two-branch norm is $2a^2$. Since $a\ne0$, cancellation gives $a^2/(2a^2)=1/2$ on both coordinates.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Equal-Amplitude Observed Probabilities]
Under the same hypotheses,
\[
 \Pobs(s\mid\phi)=\Pobs(t\mid\phi)=\frac12.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The exact weights are the embedded real number $1/2$, which standard part fixes.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Equal-Amplitude Obstruction]
If $\varepsilon$ is a nonzero infinitesimal surreal label, then
\[
 \frac{1}{\sqrt2}|0\rangle+\frac{1}{\sqrt2}|\varepsilon\rangle
\]
has observed probability $1/2$ at each outcome. An infinitesimal label alone cannot make its branch unobservable.
\end{corollary}

The arithmetic magnitude, sign, birthday, and infinitesimal status of a label do not enter the probability calculation. A model that wishes a label's magnitude to influence likelihood must encode that dependence in amplitudes or in a separate observable.

\section{The epsilon-amplitude experiment}
Let $\varepsilon\in\Rstar$ be a positive infinitesimal and set
\[
 |\psi_\varepsilon\rangle=|0\rangle+\varepsilon|1\rangle.
\]
Here the labels are ordinary and the second amplitude is infinitesimal.

\begin{proposition}[Exact Epsilon Weights]
The squared norm and exact weights are
\[
 N(\psi_\varepsilon)=1+\varepsilon^2,
\quad
 \Pexact(0\mid\psi_\varepsilon)=\frac{1}{1+\varepsilon^2},
\quad
 \Pexact(1\mid\psi_\varepsilon)=\frac{\varepsilon^2}{1+\varepsilon^2}.
\]
The two weights sum exactly to one.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Apply the two-branch norm with amplitudes $1$ and $\varepsilon$, then substitute into the definition. Their sum is $(1+\varepsilon^2)/(1+\varepsilon^2)=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Epsilon Collapse]
The observed probabilities satisfy
\[
 \Pobs(0\mid\psi_\varepsilon)=1,
 \qquad
 \Pobs(1\mid\psi_\varepsilon)=0.
\]
Consequently, their sum is one.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The quantity $\varepsilon^2$ is infinitesimal, so $1+\varepsilon^2$ and its inverse have standard part one. The first exact weight therefore has standard part one. The second has an infinitesimal numerator and appreciable denominator, so the unobservability theorem gives zero.
\end{proof}

A real numerical family $\varepsilon_k=10^{-k}$ illustrates the shadow:
\[
 P_k(0)=\frac{1}{1+10^{-2k}},\qquad
 P_k(1)=\frac{10^{-2k}}{1+10^{-2k}}.
\]
These tend to $1$ and $0$. The sequence is illustrative only: standard part acts on a fixed nonzero infinitesimal rather than replacing it by a real limit.

\section{A lexicographic probability model}
Let $L=\mathbb Q\times\mathbb Q$, interpret $(a,b)$ as $a+b\delta$, and order pairs lexicographically. Addition is coordinatewise.

\begin{definition}[Lexicographic standard part]
Define $\st_L(a,b)=a$. This map is additive.
\end{definition}

Fix $n\ge0$ and let $\Omega_n=\{\bot,1,\ldots,n\}$. Give each visible atom $i$ weight
\[
 w(i)=(0,1)=\delta,
\]
and give the reservoir atom weight
\[
 w(\bot)=(1,-n)=1-n\delta.
\]
For $A\subseteq\Omega_n$, define $\mu_n(A)=\sum_{x\in A}w(x)$. If $A$ contains $k$ visible atoms, then
\[
 \mu_n(A)=
 \begin{cases}
 (0,k),&\bot\notin A,\\
 (1,k-n),&\bot\in A.
 \end{cases}
\]
The whole space has exact mass $(1,0)$, and $\mu_n$ is finitely additive on disjoint events.

\begin{theorem}[Dirac Collapse]
For every event $A\subseteq\Omega_n$,
\[
 \st_L(\mu_n(A))=
 \begin{cases}
 1,&\bot\in A,\\
 0,&\bot\notin A.
 \end{cases}
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
In the displayed closed form, the first coordinate is one exactly when the reservoir belongs to the event.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Normalization and Invisible Atoms]
The observed total mass is one, while each visible singleton has observed mass zero:
\[
 \st_L(\mu_n(\Omega_n))=1,
 \qquad
 \st_L(\mu_n(\{i\}))=0.
\]
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Observed Finite Additivity]
If $A\cap B=\varnothing$, then
\[
 \st_L(\mu_n(A\cup B))
 =\st_L(\mu_n(A))+\st_L(\mu_n(B)).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Exact finite additivity holds in $L$, and first-coordinate projection is additive. Equivalently, two disjoint events cannot both contain the unique reservoir atom.
\end{proof}

The observed measure is exactly the Dirac measure $\delta_\bot$. Positive infinitesimal visible masses survive in the exact model but disappear from its real shadow.

\section{Structural bridge}
The quantum and discrete constructions have the same architecture. Hyperreal Born weights and lexicographic rational weights are exact values; squared infinitesimal amplitudes and second-coordinate atom masses hold lower-order data; standard part and first-coordinate projection are observation maps. Exact normalization survives, and infinitesimal components vanish from the classical shadow.

The analogy also identifies the stabilizing hypothesis. In the quantum theorem, an appreciable norm prevents normalization from magnifying an infinitesimal numerator. In the discrete model, the reservoir already has dominant coordinate one. Both fix the leading scale before observation.

\section{Algorithms}
\begin{algorithm}[Finite Observed Born Distribution]
Given distinct labels $s_1,\ldots,s_m$, amplitudes $a_1,\ldots,a_m$, and an observation map $\sigma$:
\begin{enumerate}
 \item Compute $N=\sum_{i=1}^m a_i^2$.
 \item Reject the zero state if $N=0$.
 \item Compute $w_i=a_i^2/N$.
 \item Return $p_i=\sigma(w_i)$.
 \item Check $\sum_iw_i=1$ in the exact arithmetic.
\end{enumerate}
The time cost is $O(m)$ and the output storage is $O(m)$. In the mathematical model $\sigma$ is standard part; a floating-point tolerance is only a visualization proxy.
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[Lexicographic Event Measurement]
For an event summarized by reservoir membership and the number $k$ of visible atoms, return $(0,k)$ and observed value $0$ if the reservoir is absent; otherwise return $(1,k-n)$ and observed value $1$. The summary computation is $O(1)$. If the event arrives as a list, counting takes $O(|A|)$.
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[Observational Equivalence]
Align the finite supports of two states, compute their observed Born distributions, and compare every coordinate. The states are observationally equivalent exactly when all observed weights agree. Once supports are aligned, the cost is linear in their combined size.
\end{algorithm}

\section{Applications, limitations, and future work}
Non-Archimedean weights provide exact priority layers: order-one mass dominates order-$\varepsilon$ mass, which dominates order-$\varepsilon^2$ mass, without choosing a numerical cutoff. This is relevant to perturbative sectors, asymptotic models, lexicographic decisions, and any setting where a lower-order distinction should be retained internally but omitted from leading observations.

The framework also prevents a common interpretive error. Extreme labels do not automatically imply extreme statistics. An infinite, infinitesimal, or otherwise exotic outcome label changes the identity of an outcome, not its probability. Statistical suppression must enter through amplitudes or an explicitly label-sensitive observable.

The present construction is finite-support and uses an ordered scalar field. Conventional quantum theory additionally uses complex amplitudes, conjugate-linear inner products, complete normed spaces, and self-adjoint operators. A broader theory must choose compatible notions of complexification, positivity, completeness, and spectral measure. No general spectral theorem is claimed here.

The immediate next result should establish that every finite state with finite amplitudes and appreciable nonzero norm produces, after standard part, a nonnegative real distribution of total mass one. Observational equivalence should then be studied as a quotient, including compatibility with relabelling and tensoring by appreciably normalized ancillary states. In finite dimensions, symmetric matrices over real-closed non-Archimedean fields suggest an algebraic spectral theorem, with additional finiteness hypotheses needed for eigenvalues to descend under standard part. Finally, replacing pairs $(a,b)$ by lexicographic towers $(a_0,\ldots,a_k)$ would create a hierarchy of observational resolutions.

\section{Conclusion}
Finite surreal-labelled states with hyperreal amplitudes support an exact normalized Born rule. Standard part converts exact weights into real observations. Under an appreciable total scale, infinitesimal amplitudes generate invisible branches. Equal amplitudes instead produce equal probabilities independently of whether a ket label is ordinary, infinite, or infinitesimal. Thus infinitesimal unobservability belongs to normalized amplitude, not to the arithmetic size of the label.

The lexicographic model confirms the same principle: infinitesimal atom masses vanish under projection to the dominant coordinate, leaving a normalized, finitely additive Dirac measure. Standard part is therefore a controlled observation map that preserves leading probability while forgetting exact lower-order distinctions.

\end{document}

